  .itm-pg-picker__sort-btn.is-active { color: #131313; font-weight: 900; }
  .itm-pg-picker__sort-arrow { font-size: 9px; color: var(--mega-accent, #BD2321); }

  {%- comment -%}
    The sticky left/right zones. --pg-row-bg is set once per <li> (see the
    zebra striping below) and inherited automatically by everything inside
    it — including these — so the sticky cells always match their own
    row's white/grey shade rather than going transparent and letting
    scrolled-past spec columns show through underneath them.

    left/right MUST be 0, not 16px — 16px was tried first for breathing
    room, but that left the strip from the true scroll edge (0) to 16px
    completely uncovered by this box, so whatever was scrolling underneath
    showed straight through it. The 16px gutter is padding-left/right on
    the box ITSELF instead, so the opaque background covers all the way to
    the true edge and the text is simply inset within that fully-covered
    box — nothing to peek out from behind.

    A solid border (not a soft box-shadow) marks the scroll boundary —
    concrete "this is the edge" line, not a gradient that visually eats
    into whatever text sits just past it.

    No margin-left: auto on the sticky block — sticky positioning combined
    with an auto margin is unreliable (this is exactly why Price wasn't
    actually stopping when scrolled: the auto margin confused the sticky
    calculation for the whole block, even though it looked fine when the
    table happened to be scrolled all the way).

    padding-top/bottom: 12px + display: flex; align-items: center — the
    row/head no longer carry any vertical padding of their own (see
    align-items: stretch above), so every sticky box needs that padding
    on ITSELF: it's what makes the box (and so its border-right/left and
    background) reach the row's true full height with zero gap top or
    bottom, closing the "line stops short of the row edges" bug. Centring
    the text back inside that now-taller box is what "display: flex;
    align-items: center" on .itm-pg-picker__name is for — a plain <a>
    otherwise just sits its text at the top of whatever height stretch
    gives it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    max-width: 210px here is NOT decorative — .itm-pg-picker__sort-btn--name
    also carries the plain .itm-pg-picker__sort-btn class (for its shared
    button/font/hover styling), which sets max-width: 150px for every
    OTHER header column. Without overriding it back to 210px here too,
    flex: 0 0 210px is silently capped down to 150px on the header only
    (a <span>/<a> in the body rows never had that class, so it always
    rendered the full 210px) — the exact reason the divider line sat
    further left in the header than in the rows below it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .itm-pg-picker__sort-btn--name,
  .itm-pg-picker__name {
    position: sticky;
    left: 0;
    z-index: 3;
    flex: 0 0 210px;
    max-width: 210px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 12px 0 12px 16px;
    background: var(--pg-row-bg, #ffffff);
    border-right: 1px solid #d8d8d8;
  }
  {%- comment -%}
    flex: 0 0 261px (a fixed number), not "auto" — auto sizes this box to
    fit its own content, and relying on the header's two buttons
    (90 + 12 gap + 130) happening to measure out to the exact same pixel
    width as the row's price span + Add to Cart button (90 + 12 + 130) is
    exactly the kind of thing that only needs one tiny rendering
    difference — different element type, font kerning, a shorter price
    string — to drift a few px and misalign the two dividers. A fixed
    number removes that risk entirely, the same way every other column
    here already uses a fixed width instead of shrink-to-fit — see also
    the min-width: 0 added to .itm-pg-picker__actions below, which closes
    the actual gap that was causing that drift in practice.

    261 = 232 content (90 + 12 gap + 130, fixed/non-shrinking children)
    + 28 padding (12 left + 16 right, below) + 1 border-left — this theme
    is box-sizing: border-box sitewide, so the box's declared width has
    to include padding/border, not just its content.

    padding: 12px 16px 12px 12px — 16px on the right (against the
    scrollport edge, matching the Name column's own left gutter) and now
    12px on the LEFT too, so the border-left line has breathing room
    before the price/actions content instead of sitting flush against it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .itm-pg-picker__list-head-end,
  .itm-pg-picker__row-end {
    position: sticky;
    right: 0;
    z-index: 3;
    flex: 0 0 261px;
    padding: 12px 16px 12px 12px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 12px;
    background: var(--pg-row-bg, #ffffff);
    border-left: 1px solid #d8d8d8;
  }
  {%- comment -%}
    padding-top/bottom: 0 on both — they sit inside .itm-pg-picker__list-head-end,
    which already carries its own 12px top/bottom padding (above); without
    this reset they'd ALSO keep the base .itm-pg-picker__sort-btn rule's
    12px top/bottom, doubling up to 24px and making this one column taller
    than every other header button.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .itm-pg-picker__sort-btn--price { flex: 0 0 90px; padding-top: 0; padding-bottom: 0; }
  .itm-pg-picker__sort-btn--actions { flex: 0 0 130px; padding-top: 0; padding-bottom: 0; cursor: default; }

  {%- comment -%}
    Floating scrollbar — [hidden] by default (see the markup) and toggled
