  .itm-pg-picker__floating-scroll[hidden] { display: none; }
  .itm-pg-picker__floating-scroll-track {
    {%- comment -%} Height only — width is set in JS to match the real table's scrollWidth, so the two thumbs are always the same proportional size. {%- endcomment -%}
    height: 1px;
  }

  {%- comment -%}
    Closes the table into a neat bordered box on desktop — left/right
    borders on the wrap itself (the header's own border-bottom already
    draws the line under "Model / Size / .../ Price", and the top/bottom
    edges of the box come from the wrap's border-top plus the last row's
    own border-bottom below).
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    No overflow: hidden here — mismatched overflow-x/overflow-y values on
    the same element (auto + hidden) is exactly the kind of thing that
    makes position: sticky behave unreliably, which is what was happening.
    The rounded-corner look this used to get from clipping is done by
    rounding the header's own top corners and the last row's own bottom
    corners directly instead — same result, no clipping needed at all.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  .itm-pg-picker__list-wrap {
    border: 1px solid #e2e2e2;
    border-radius: 6px;
  }
  .itm-pg-picker__list { border-top: none; }
  .itm-pg-picker__item:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .itm-pg-picker__item:last-child .itm-pg-picker__row { border-radius: 0 0 5px 5px; }
  {%- comment -%}
    Zebra striping — exactly two flat shades total (white / grey). The
    specs block (mobile only) has no background of its own — it shows
    whatever colour its parent .itm-pg-picker__item already is, so the
    whole item reads as one solid band.

    width: max-content + min-width: 100% here too, matching .itm-pg-picker
    __row/__list-head — without it, the LI itself stays at its ordinary
    block width (the visible viewport) while its .row child grows wider to
    fit every spec column, so the row's content visibly overflowed the LI
    with nothing behind it: no zebra background, no border-bottom, past
